  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1107 AM MDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nMOSTLY DRY AND WARM THROUGH SATURDAY:\n\nA weak shortwave is currently moving through the area this \nmorning with isolated shower activity across eastern Utah into \nnorthwest Colorado. Lower levels still remain dry so not \nexpecting much rain to reach the ground with gusty winds the \nprimary concern. Hi res guidance is indicating isolated shower \nand storm activity this afternoon focused over the high terrain \nwith more scattered coverage across the Four Corners. High \npressure remains south of the Four Corners through Saturday with\nsome drier air and more isolated coverage of afternoon \nconvection over the high terrain with gusty winds the primary \nconcern. The flow turns southwesterly on Saturday with breezier \nconditions as a low pressure trough moves into the Pacific \nNorthwest. As this happens, relative humidity will increase \nenough to negate critical fire weather conditions even though \nwinds increase. Still localized critical fire weather conditions\nare possible this afternoon and Saturday afternoon. Aside from \nthe isolated shower activity and cloud cover, conditions through\nSaturday will remain mostly dry and warm with highs 5 to 10 \ndegrees above normal. \n\nCOOLER AND WETTER PATTERN SUNDAY INTO NEXT WEEK:\n\nMoisture begins to increase more on Sunday as the Pacific \nNorthwest low moves inland across the PacNW and Intermountain \nWest, with the high pressure ridge shifting east allowing better\nmoisture to advect from the southwest. A surface cold front \nlooks to move through Sunday night so shower activity is \nexpected to pick up late Sunday into Monday with overnight \nconvection possible. This pattern looks to remain in place \nthrough much of the coming week with troughiness across the \nPacific Northwest and Intermountain West and the high positioned\nto our southeast across the SE U.S., keeping our CWA in that \nfavorable moist southwesterly flow. We will have a direct tap to\nthis abundant moisture as PWAT anomalies climb up towards 180 \nto 200 percent of normal late Sunday and remain overhead through\nat least mid next week. A robust upper level jet will remain \nparked to our northwest, keeping that dynamic forcing in place \neven though no well defined shortwaves are seen. So Sunday \nthrough at least mid next week will remain unsettled with more \nwidespread coverage of showers with thunderstorms also possible.\nTemperatures will also cool as a result of the increased \nmoisture by Monday through the coming week with highs dropping \ncloser to normal. This is looking like a cool, wet pattern more \nreminiscent of fall and wouldn't be surprised to see a dusting \nof snow at the higher peaks with any overnight precipitation.",
